{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":false,"decision":"fail","reasons":["security.status_not_clean"],"slug":"bilibili-all-in-one","displayName":"Bilibili All In One","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wscats/skills/bilibili-all-in-one","publisherHandle":"wscats","publisherDisplayName":"enoyao","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wscats","version":"1.0.24","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1777702888540,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/bilibili-all-in-one/card?ownerHandle=wscats&version=1.0.24","sha256":"e9f1d50f5f1dfacef4829bedb366ac6cd69a72e0e5f78d91ae58077bc55f3f33","size":2516,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"2f9d74ee9a706aff8a010212cde558f163021ba3cd76003fb5ddd6e3b4a89a40","bundleFingerprints":["bac5306ac3fcd4bfca930b77d19338e55304394d45c8a7affc8f43869ed7c18b","88b5fd6d7f40ebd2c51ceaa198b0f20383076e4564ce96fa761c458299ce7806"],"files":[{"path":"README.md","size":17017,"sha256":"be2dc06a09668cd48d1ce283dc6a6d0385a30f6804027febc8fd4a4109464d79","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"STRUCTURE.md","size":794,"sha256":"746788f6f129ee1df11fbc14f2fa857517866c0349c63272bf0a863c5bf03b5e","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"main.py","size":5372,"sha256":"687bd370db791ee5a5bd6f6616fa5204fdb6075673de5446ece0270eb40361a7","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"requirements.txt","size":83,"sha256":"0708a4e28dcc7616f1b083d8f8d3726cde05c7a5e6a641876971cb8d11f80054","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/publish.sh","size":3758,"sha256":"c739b8d18655a74e5882e9a7f97df0a962ecc62aa459ad363ead6f5fcae01b58","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill.json","size":18244,"sha256":"bef4b0f61b5a740a827250f21c5937f03bbf981e3cba0c37287ef6cab04a58c0","contentType":"application/json"},{"path":"skill.md","size":21374,"sha256":"92602328d62540c6753850733f4986cb870bef5372f60cccf86a6b350dc96d5d","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"src/__init__.py","size":515,"sha256":"da3198962dce28345eb200ed17e1186a9767c760737254ff835083193f840444","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/auth.py","size":11550,"sha256":"04b3cb0cdf681f54e691a8add73c6ef6734f53eda1d86da032133f8b945fb2ae","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/downloader.py","size":18913,"sha256":"254a69d15c9f08000768c251aac9c299c1c9395d5125211570b3ced5b022980d","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/hot_monitor.py","size":7391,"sha256":"3b7fc7dc4af3cb8dad5b1a92b7b23de0b73ec0e852f29649b9491ec1b7f7f6cf","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/player.py","size":12167,"sha256":"fb18175aede2ddb10c17ac79a175b99b874add6e4f706da483556ee5aae1ff4e","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/publisher.py","size":26526,"sha256":"3fd2548bb127376ece2b53b3af759639ca102154e6de5567c5b8a9f900f48ab4","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/subtitle.py","size":24254,"sha256":"73dfb3ea42d011fb65907fb71e8e93f2a922856c15547c3b949d2e750f0f46ea","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/utils.py","size":7847,"sha256":"d4ac2f350651e4f3259edf0d27b122c7c7c6cd2a4d41300795de1ae92363f7f8","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/watcher.py","size":9896,"sha256":"742a5d1e80d6e4c458fb8347f5d1c288fe77b0953b6c9bd87c9d0207d0a89982","contentType":"text/plain"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"suspicious","passed":false,"rawStatus":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill is a real Bilibili toolkit, but users should review it because full session cookies can be attached to broader network requests than the documentation promises.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789177759516,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T09","level":"error","message":{"text":"Full Session Cookies Sent to an Unvalidated API-Provided Subtitle URL"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"src/subtitle.py"},"region":{"startLine":158,"endLine":164}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"432f2f101a576fb3"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `src/auth.py:196-207`, `src/subtitle.py:158-164`, `main.py:52-61`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Sensitive credential disclosure through an unvalidated outbound request  \n**Risk Level**: High\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n`src/auth.py:196-207` creates a client that attaches all available Bilibili session cookies to its requests and automatically follows redirects:\n\n```python\ndef get_client(self) -> httpx.AsyncClient:\n    \"\"\"Create an authenticated async HTTP client.\n\n    Returns:\n        httpx.AsyncClient configured with credentials.\n    \"\"\"\n    return httpx.AsyncClient(\n        headers=self.get_headers(),\n        cookies=self.cookies,\n        timeout=30.0,\n        follow_redirects=True,\n    )\n```\n\n`main.py:52-61` supplies this authentication object to the subtitle module even though public subtitle operations do not normally require authentication:\n\n```python\n# Initialize all modules\nself.hot_monitor = HotMonitor(auth=self.auth)\nself.downloader = BilibiliDownloader(auth=self.auth)\nself.watcher = BilibiliWatcher(auth=self.auth)\nself.player = BilibiliPlayer(auth=self.auth)\nself.subtitle = SubtitleDownloader(\n    auth=self.auth,\n    downloader=self.downloader,\n    player=self.player,\n)\n```\n\n`src/subtitle.py:158-164` takes a URL returned by the remote API and requests it through the authenticated client without validating the destination:\n\n```python\n# Download subtitle JSON\nsub_url = target_sub[\"url\"]\nif sub_url.startswith(\"//\"):\n    sub_url = \"https:\" + sub_url\n\nasync with self._get_client() as client:\n    resp = await client.get(sub_url)\n    sub_data = resp.json()\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe authentication client is initialized with the complete cookie set returned by `self.cookies`, including `SESSDATA`, `bili_jct`, and `buvid3`. These are full browser-session credentials rather than narrowly scoped API tokens.\n\nThe subtitle URL originates in the Bilibili subtitle metadata response and is not checked against an HTTPS hostname allowlist before it is requested. Because the same client carries authentication cookies, a malicious or compromised API response can direct the request to an unintended host and cause session credentials to accompany that request.\n\nAutomatic redirect handling increases the exposure surface. The code does not disable redirects or independently validate each redirect destination. The design also exceeds least privilege because the Skill documentation states that subtitle listing and downloading generally work without authentication.\n\nThe issue does not establish intentional credential exfiltration. It is an insecure client-boundary design that creates a credential-disclosure path if the API response or redirect chain is maliciously influenced.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. A user initializes the Skill with valid `SESSDATA`, `bili_jct`, and optionally `buvid3`.\n2. `main.py` passes the authenticated object to `SubtitleDownloader`.\n3. The user invokes the subtitle download action for a video.\n4. The subtitle metadata API returns a `subtitle_url`.\n5. An attacker who compromises or manipulates the API response, relevant upstream infrastructure, or redirect chain causes that URL to reference an attacker-controlled destination.\n6. `SubtitleDownloader.download()` requests the supplied URL using `BilibiliAuth.get_client()`.\n7. The client sends the request with the broadly configured session-cookie jar and follows redirects.\n8. The attacker captures the disclosed cookie values and attempts to replay them against Bilibili.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nSuccessful exploitation can disclose full Bilibili session cookies. Depending on the account state and Bilibili's server-side controls, an attacker may be able to:\n\n- Impersonate the authenticated user.\n- Read account-specific information.\n- Access authenticated or high-quality playback resources.\n- Perform account operations available to the stolen session.\n- Combine `SESSDATA` with the disclosed CSRF cookie to attempt authenticated write operations.\n- Retain access until the session is revoked, expires, or is invalidated.\n\nThe vulnerability affects users who provide Bilibili credentials. Unauthenticated users are not exposed to session-cookie theft through this path.\n  ]]>","severity":"High"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Use a dedicated unauthenticated client to download subtitle content:\n   ```python\n   async with httpx.AsyncClient(\n       headers=DEFAULT_HEADERS,\n       timeout=30.0,\n       follow_redirects=False,\n   ) as client:\n       resp = await client.get(sub_url)\n   ```\n\n2. Validate subtitle URLs before making a request:\n   - Require the `https` scheme.\n   - Reject embedded credentials and nonstandard ports.\n   - Compare the normalized hostname against a narrow allowlist of documented Bilibili subtitle/CDN domains.\n   - Reject IP literals, loopback addresses, private networks, and link-local destinations unless explicitly required.\n\n3. Disable automatic redirects for sensitive clients. If redirects are operationally required, process them manually and apply the same scheme and hostname validation to every destination.\n\n4. Do not inject authenticated clients into public read-only modules by default. Create an authenticated client only for actions and endpoints that demonstrably require authentication.\n\n5. Scope cookies to the exact official domains that require them rather than installing domainless cookies on a general-purpose HTTP client.\n\n6. Add tests confirming that:\n   - Subtitle-content requests carry no session cookies.\n   - Unapproved hosts and schemes are rejected.\n   - Cross-domain redirects are rejected.\n   - Public subtitle operations work when credentials are absent.\n  ]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"T09","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Bilibili Session Cookies Unnecessarily Transmitted to the Upload CDN"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"src/publisher.py"},"region":{"startLine":571,"endLine":649}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"eca0d513ce53e7cf"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `src/auth.py:196-207`, `src/publisher.py:571-649`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Excessive credential propagation and failure to enforce least privilege  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n`src/auth.py:196-207` creates a general-purpose client containing the user's full session-cookie set:\n\n```python\ndef get_client(self) -> httpx.AsyncClient:\n    \"\"\"Create an authenticated async HTTP client.\n\n    Returns:\n        httpx.AsyncClient configured with credentials.\n    \"\"\"\n    return httpx.AsyncClient(\n        headers=self.get_headers(),\n        cookies=self.cookies,\n        timeout=30.0,\n        follow_redirects=True,\n    )\n```\n\n`src/publisher.py:571-649` uses that authenticated client for upload-CDN requests even though the upload is separately authorized using `X-Upos-Auth`:\n\n```python\nupos_uri = preupload[\"upos_uri\"]\nauth = preupload.get(\"auth\")\nbiz_id = preupload.get(\"biz_id\")\nchunk_size = preupload.get(\"chunk_size\", 4 * 1024 * 1024)\nfile_size = preupload[\"file_size\"]\n\n# Extract upos key from URI\nupos_key = upos_uri.replace(\"upos://\", \"\")\nfilename = upos_key.split(\"/\")[-1].split(\".\")[0]\n\n# Calculate chunk count\nchunk_count = (file_size + chunk_size - 1) // chunk_size\n\n# Init upload\nupload_base = f\"https://upos-sz-upcdnbda2.bilivideo.com/{upos_key}\"\n\nasync with self._get_client() as client:\n    # Fetch upload ID\n    resp = await client.post(\n        upload_base,\n        params={\n            \"uploads\": \"\",\n            \"output\": \"json\",\n        },\n        headers={\"X-Upos-Auth\": auth} if auth else {},\n    )\n\n    try:\n        init_data = resp.json()\n        upload_id = init_data.get(\"upload_id\", \"\")\n    except Exception:\n        return {\"success\": False, \"message\": \"Failed to initialize upload\"}\n\n# Upload chunks\nwith open(file_path, \"rb\") as f:\n    for chunk_idx in range(chunk_count):\n        chunk_data = f.read(chunk_size)\n        start = chunk_idx * chunk_size\n        end = min(start + len(chunk_data), file_size)\n\n        async with self._get_client() as client:\n            resp = await client.put(\n                upload_base,\n                params={\n                    \"partNumber\": chunk_idx + 1,\n                    \"uploadId\": upload_id,\n                    \"chunk\": chunk_idx,\n                    \"chunks\": chunk_count,\n                    \"size\": len(chunk_data),\n                    \"start\": start,\n                    \"end\": end,\n                    \"total\": file_size,\n                },\n                headers={\n                    \"X-Upos-Auth\": auth or \"\",\n                    \"Content-Type\": \"application/octet-stream\",\n                },\n                content=chunk_data,\n            )\n\n            if resp.status_code not in (200, 202):\n                return {\n                    \"success\": False,\n                    \"message\": f\"Upload chunk {chunk_idx + 1}/{chunk_count} failed\",\n                }\n\n# Complete upload\nparts = [{\"partNumber\": i + 1, \"eTag\": \"etag\"} for i in range(chunk_count)]\n\nasync with self._get_client() as client:\n    resp = await client.post(\n        upload_base,\n        params={\n            \"output\": \"json\",\n            \"name\": preupload[\"file_name\"],\n            \"profile\": \"ugcupos/bup\",\n            \"uploadId\": upload_id,\n            \"biz_id\": biz_id or 0,\n        },\n        json={\"parts\": parts},\n        headers={\"X-Upos-Auth\": auth or \"\"},\n    )\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe upload process obtains a dedicated upload authorization value from the pre-upload response and supplies it through the `X-Upos-Auth` header. Nevertheless, every initialization, chunk upload, and completion request is sent through `self._get_client()`, which contains the user's full Bilibili session cookies.\n\nThe upload CDN does not need broad account session credentials when the dedicated upload token is sufficient. Sending both credential types violates least privilege and unnecessarily expands the number of services that receive replayable session secrets. A CDN request may also follow redirects because the shared client enables `follow_redirects=True`.\n\nThe destination is a documented Bilibili-owned upload domain, so this is not evidence of deliberate third-party exfiltration. The security issue is unnecessary secret propagation across service boundaries, increasing exposure to CDN logging, infrastructure compromise, redirect misconfiguration, or unrelated service vulnerabilities.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. A user supplies valid Bilibili session cookies and explicitly confirms a publishing operation.\n2. The publisher calls the official pre-upload endpoint and receives an upload URI and `X-Upos-Auth` token.\n3. `_upload_file()` constructs the fixed Bilibili upload-CDN URL.\n4. The publisher initializes, uploads chunks, and completes the upload using `self._get_client()`.\n5. The shared client includes the user's complete Bilibili session-cookie set in addition to the dedicated upload authorization token.\n6. A compromised CDN component, logging layer, reverse proxy, or malicious redirect destination captures the unnecessary cookie values.\n7. An attacker attempts to replay the cookies against account-facing Bilibili services.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe unnecessarily exposed secrets are full account session cookies, not upload-only credentials. If captured and accepted for replay, they may provide broader privileges than the upload operation itself, including authenticated account access and potentially write operations when combined with the CSRF value.\n\nThe operation is protected by a dry-run default and requires `dry_run=False` with `confirm=True`, which reduces accidental publishing. However, those controls do not mitigate credential exposure once a legitimate upload is confirmed.\n  ]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Create a separate cookie-free client for upload-CDN operations:\n   ```python\n   async with httpx.AsyncClient(\n       headers=DEFAULT_HEADERS,\n       timeout=30.0,\n       follow_redirects=False,\n       cookies=None,\n   ) as client:\n       resp = await client.put(\n           upload_base,\n           headers={\n               \"X-Upos-Auth\": auth or \"\",\n               \"Content-Type\": \"application/octet-stream\",\n           },\n           content=chunk_data,\n           params=upload_params,\n       )\n   ```\n\n2. Reserve the session-cookie client for account-facing API endpoints that explicitly require session authentication.\n\n3. Disable redirects on upload requests. If redirects are required by Bilibili's protocol, manually validate each destination against a narrow upload-CDN allowlist before resending the request.\n\n4. Validate `upos_uri` before incorporating it into a URL:\n   - Require the expected `upos://` prefix.\n   - Reject traversal sequences, query delimiters, fragments, control characters, and malformed path components.\n   - Keep the upload hostname fixed or choose it only from a trusted endpoint allowlist.\n\n5. Prefer a single cookie-free upload client for the complete multipart operation rather than repeatedly constructing authenticated clients for every chunk.\n\n6. Add automated tests that inspect outbound requests and assert that `SESSDATA`, `bili_jct`, and `buvid3` are absent from all upload-CDN requests while `X-Upos-Auth` remains present.\n  ]]>"}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":35,"primaryLanguage":"Python","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789177582.815949,"endTime":1789177690.6490507}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"bilibili-all-in-one","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v579ws6sxg3xjzxdpszn5x3ba58e8sdm-TEAXmC/clawscan-skillspector-2341815721/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-12T01:46:21.725340+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","max_issue_severity":"HIGH"},"components":[{"path":"README.md","type":"markdown","lines":507,"executable":false,"size_bytes":17017,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"STRUCTURE.md","type":"markdown","lines":16,"executable":false,"size_bytes":794,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"main.py","type":"python","lines":158,"executable":true,"size_bytes":5372,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"requirements.txt","type":"text","lines":5,"executable":false,"size_bytes":83,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"scripts/publish.sh","type":"shell","lines":106,"executable":true,"size_bytes":3758,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"skill.json","type":"json","lines":418,"executable":false,"size_bytes":18244,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"skill.md","type":"markdown","lines":606,"executable":false,"size_bytes":21374,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/__init__.py","type":"python","lines":19,"executable":true,"size_bytes":515,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/auth.py","type":"python","lines":310,"executable":true,"size_bytes":11550,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/downloader.py","type":"python","lines":527,"executable":true,"size_bytes":18913,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/hot_monitor.py","type":"python","lines":241,"executable":true,"size_bytes":7391,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/player.py","type":"python","lines":390,"executable":true,"size_bytes":12167,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/publisher.py","type":"python","lines":786,"executable":true,"size_bytes":26526,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/subtitle.py","type":"python","lines":730,"executable":true,"size_bytes":24254,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/utils.py","type":"python","lines":275,"executable":true,"size_bytes":7847,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"src/watcher.py","type":"python","lines":310,"executable":true,"size_bytes":9896,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-8ec147a04a64426aa12dc98bb2618676","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":78,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"The README instructs users to place active Bilibili session cookies in a local credentials.json file. Storing reusable authentication material in a plaintext file materially increases the risk of credential theft via local malware, accidental commits, backups, shell history, or other users on the same machine, especially because these cookies enable authenticated actions such as publishing or high-quality downloads.","remediation":"Prefer ephemeral credentials via environment variables or secure OS keychain/secret manager integration instead of plaintext JSON files. If file-based storage must be supported, place it outside the project tree, use restrictive permissions by default, add the filename to .gitignore, warn prominently against committing it, and support encryption or secure secret backends.","code_snippet":"\n### 方式二：凭据文件\n\n创建 `credentials.json`：\n\n```json\n{","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":78,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-4e09c40a2f704e8e8e88c9e616581c9d","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.94,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":121,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"The documented optional persistence feature automatically saves sensitive Bilibili cookies to .credentials.json and reloads them on startup. Even with 0600 permissions, disk persistence expands the attack surface by leaving long-lived session material available to local compromise, backups, accidental inclusion in archives, or unsafe workspace sharing; in this skill context the impact is elevated because the same credentials can authorize account actions like video upload and editing.","remediation":"Disable automatic credential persistence by default and require explicit, informed opt-in with prominent warnings. Store secrets in a platform credential store where possible; otherwise keep the file outside the repository, ensure it is ignored by version control, minimize retention, provide rotation/revocation guidance, and document exactly when the file is created and deleted.","code_snippet":"app.auth.clear_persisted()\n```\n\n启用后，凭据自动保存到项目根目录的 `.credentials.json`（权限 `0600`，仅所有者可读写），下次启动时自动加载。\n\n> 💡 **如何获取 Cookie？** 登录 [bilibili.com](https://www.bilibili.com) → 按 F12 打开开发者工具 → Application → Cookies → 复制 `SESSDATA`、`bili_jct`、`buvid3` 的值。\n","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":121,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TP4","finding_id":"finding-9a90b785f1c74d4ab446cae88f347ac7","category":"MCP Tool Poisoning","pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"Yes, this is a mismatch. The declared description is about end-user Bilibili features: trending, downloading, playback, subtitles, and video publishing to Bilibili. The actual code chunk does none of those things. Instead, it is a developer-facing release helper that bumps a semantic version in skill.json and runs `clawhub publish` to publish the skill itself. That is a materially different purpose and uses different resources than declared. Although the comment notes this script is excluded from the shipped package, the supplied code chunk itself still does not implement the described Bilibili functionality.","remediation":"Update the skill description to accurately reflect all capabilities, or remove undeclared functionality from the implementation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02","AML.T0080"],"evidence":{"code_path":"scripts/publish.sh","code_start_line":1,"code_end_line":106,"actual_behavior_summary":"The supplied code is a local release automation script that reads and updates the skill's version in skill.json and invokes the clawhub CLI to publish the skill package to Clawhub."},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TP4","finding_id":"finding-8cb37cefe7894c0e9e2d114eaadd135d","category":"MCP Tool Poisoning","pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"The supplied code is specifically a downloader module (`src/downloader.py`). It supports getting video info, enumerating formats, downloading single or multiple videos, selecting DASH audio/video streams, and merging them with ffmpeg. It does not implement trending monitoring, playback, subtitle download, or publishing in this chunk. There is no evidence here of upload/publish flows or subtitle APIs. While the code is consistent with part of the declared description (video downloading, optional auth integration, HTTPS requests to Bilibili endpoints), the declared purpose materially overstates what this supplied code chunk actually does. This is a description-versus-code mismatch for the chunk provided.","remediation":"Update the skill description to accurately reflect all capabilities, or remove undeclared functionality from the implementation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02","AML.T0080"],"evidence":{"code_path":"src/downloader.py","code_start_line":1,"code_end_line":527,"actual_behavior_summary":"This code chunk implements only the downloading portion: fetching video metadata, listing available formats/qualities, downloading video/audio streams, batch downloads, and muxing with local ffmpeg. It uses Bilibili API endpoints for metadata/play URLs and downloads stream URLs over HTTPS, optionally using auth-provided HTTP clients."},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TP4","finding_id":"finding-700a0ca228c842dc96ac43109871734b","category":"MCP Tool Poisoning","pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"The code shown is narrowly focused on monitoring and retrieving Bilibili trending/ranking data via HTTP GET requests. It does not implement downloading videos, playing/watching videos, downloading subtitles, or publishing content. While the monitoring portion matches part of the declared description, the declared purpose presents a much broader unified toolkit than this code chunk actually demonstrates. Optional auth support exists only as a client source and is not used here for publishing. Therefore the description overstates the implemented capabilities for the provided code.","remediation":"Update the skill description to accurately reflect all capabilities, or remove undeclared functionality from the implementation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02","AML.T0080"],"evidence":{"code_path":"src/hot_monitor.py","code_start_line":1,"code_end_line":241,"actual_behavior_summary":"The supplied code chunk implements only a hot/trending monitor module. It fetches hot videos, trending topics, weekly must-watch lists, and category rankings from Bilibili APIs, parses video metadata, and supports optional authenticated HTTP clients."},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TP4","finding_id":"finding-c7f6722be19f4776b56e7056650cb4a2","category":"MCP Tool Poisoning","pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.96,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"The supplied code is narrowly focused on subtitle handling rather than the full toolkit described. It does accurately include subtitle downloading from Bilibili and uses HTTPS requests via httpx to Bilibili API/URLs, so that part aligns. However, the chunk also performs additional subtitle-adjacent capabilities not stated in the description: converting and merging local subtitle files, generating subtitles through speech recognition using faster-whisper after downloading audio, and exporting danmaku as SRT-like text via a player dependency. Those are undeclared capabilities. Also, this chunk does not evidence the broader declared functions like hot trending monitoring, playback, video publishing, or general video downloading; while absence alone in a partial chunk is not necessarily a mismatch, the actual behavior here is materially narrower and includes extra fallback processing capabilities not mentioned.","remediation":"Update the skill description to accurately reflect all capabilities, or remove undeclared functionality from the implementation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02","AML.T0080"],"evidence":{"code_path":"src/subtitle.py","code_start_line":1,"code_end_line":730,"actual_behavior_summary":"This code chunk implements only subtitle-related functionality: listing available subtitles, downloading subtitle JSON from Bilibili endpoints, converting/merging subtitle files, and fallback generation of text via audio transcription or danmaku retrieval using provided downloader/player components."},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TP4","finding_id":"finding-19afb67821f34ac0b8424ac4ca750d72","category":"MCP Tool Poisoning","pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.92,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"The supplied code is narrowly focused on a watcher module. It parses Bilibili video URLs, calls video detail/info APIs, returns metadata and engagement metrics, tracks those metrics over time, and compares stats across videos. That partially matches the declared 'video watching/playback' aspect, but the declared description presents a comprehensive multi-capability toolkit including downloading, subtitles, publishing, and trending monitoring. Those capabilities are not present in this code chunk. Because the evaluation is description versus this supplied code chunk, the description overstates the functionality materially, so this should be flagged as a mismatch.","remediation":"Update the skill description to accurately reflect all capabilities, or remove undeclared functionality from the implementation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02","AML.T0080"],"evidence":{"code_path":"src/watcher.py","code_start_line":1,"code_end_line":310,"actual_behavior_summary":"This code chunk implements only the watching/stats portion: it fetches Bilibili video details and engagement stats, compares multiple videos, and tracks metrics over time via HTTPS API requests. It does not download videos/subtitles, publish videos, or monitor trending content in this chunk."},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-aa35634b2a7148f0832f98392c3d1594","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"main.py","start_line":46,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"Code accesses credential files (SSH keys, AWS credentials, etc.). This could indicate credential theft attempts.","remediation":"Remove references to credential paths. Use environment variables or secrets managers. For docs, use placeholder paths (e.g., /path/to/config). Never load .env or token files in production code paths.","code_snippet":"            credential_file: Path to JSON credential file.\n            persist: Whether to persist credentials to disk (default: False).\n                Set to True or env BILIBILI_PERSIST=1 to auto-save/load\n                credentials from .credentials.json.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.auth = BilibiliAuth(\n            sessdata=sessdata,","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"main.py","start_line":46,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-aa35634b2a7148f0832f98392c3d1594","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"skill.md","start_line":119,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"Code accesses credential files (SSH keys, AWS credentials, etc.). This could indicate credential theft attempts.","remediation":"Remove references to credential paths. Use environment variables or secrets managers. For docs, use placeholder paths (e.g., /path/to/config). Never load .env or token files in production code paths.","code_snippet":"            credential_file: Path to JSON credential file.\n            persist: Whether to persist credentials to disk (default: False).\n                Set to True or env BILIBILI_PERSIST=1 to auto-save/load\n                credentials from .credentials.json.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.auth = BilibiliAuth(\n            sessdata=sessdata,","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"skill.md","start_line":119,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-aa35634b2a7148f0832f98392c3d1594","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"src/auth.py","start_line":73,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"Code accesses credential files (SSH keys, AWS credentials, etc.). This could indicate credential theft attempts.","remediation":"Remove references to credential paths. Use environment variables or secrets managers. For docs, use placeholder paths (e.g., /path/to/config). Never load .env or token files in production code paths.","code_snippet":"            credential_file: Path to JSON credential file.\n            persist: Whether to persist credentials to disk (default: False).\n                Set to True or env BILIBILI_PERSIST=1 to auto-save/load\n                credentials from .credentials.json.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.auth = BilibiliAuth(\n            sessdata=sessdata,","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"src/auth.py","start_line":73,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SC4","finding_id":"finding-4a2f0141af8b461eb3caeaea5af380f8","category":"Supply Chain","pattern":"Known Vulnerable Dependency: aiohttp==3.10.11 — 16 advisory(ies): CVE-2026-54279 (aiohttp: Host-Only Cookies Become Domain Cookies After CookieJar Persistence); CVE-2026-34514 (AIOHTTP has CRLF injection through multipart part content type header constructi); CVE-2026-34517 (AIOHTTP has late size enforcement for non-file multipart fields causes memory Do) +13 more","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"requirements.txt","start_line":2,"end_line":null},"finding":"aiohttp==3.10.11","explanation":"The dependency is pinned to aiohttp 3.10.11, which the finding reports as affected by multiple high-severity advisories including cookie scope handling and multipart parsing issues. In a network-heavy skill that performs authenticated requests and may handle uploads/downloads, these flaws can plausibly expose session cookies, enable request/header manipulation, or cause denial of service if attacker-controlled responses or content are processed.","remediation":"Upgrade aiohttp to a version that contains fixes for the listed CVEs, then regenerate and pin the updated lock/requirements entry. Review all cookie handling, multipart upload, and untrusted content processing paths; add dependency scanning in CI and verify transitive dependencies are also updated.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["Supply Chain"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"788a7bb647c39b936027f3c078a67da9c21ebc881c507581c4289baca9779b0e","occurrences":[{"file":"requirements.txt","start_line":2,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SC4","finding_id":"finding-452f7cd755044327a761cad8e22ea68c","category":"Supply Chain","pattern":"Known Vulnerable Dependency: lxml==5.3.0 — 2 advisory(ies): CVE-2026-41066 (lxml: Default configuration of iterparse() and ETCompatXMLParser() allows XXE to); CVE-2026-41066 (lxml is a library for processing XML and HTML in the Python language. Prior to 6)","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.93,"location":{"file":"requirements.txt","start_line":4,"end_line":null},"finding":"lxml==5.3.0","explanation":"lxml 5.3.0 is flagged for XXE-related issues in parser configurations such as iterparse() and ETCompatXMLParser(). Because this skill includes HTML/XML parsing libraries and interacts with remote web content, an unsafe parser path could let crafted XML trigger external entity resolution, potentially disclosing local files, making network requests, or consuming resources.","remediation":"Upgrade lxml to a patched release and audit code to ensure XML parsing disables external entity resolution, DTD loading, and network access by default. Prefer hardened parser settings explicitly, and avoid parsing untrusted XML unless strictly necessary.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["Supply Chain"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"0563dac984a56bfe0faadcf22dac53c9a9db3fb46c3eb2cc9ee42ad4aeabffdb","occurrences":[{"file":"requirements.txt","start_line":4,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-f1ccacb897b347bba1ff7dd5170ab492","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.83,"location":{"file":"skill.json","start_line":53,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"The skill supports optional persistence of live Bilibili session cookies to a local .credentials.json file. Even with opt-in behavior and documented 0600 permissions, persisted session cookies are replayable bearer credentials, so compromise of that file could let other software act as the user on Bilibili.","remediation":"Avoid storing session cookies on disk unless absolutely necessary, and prefer ephemeral in-memory credentials. If persistence is required, encrypt the credential file with an OS-backed key store, minimize retention, clearly prompt before enabling it, and ensure the implementation enforces strict file and directory permission checks.","code_snippet":"    {\n      \"name\": \"BILIBILI_PERSIST\",\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"Set to '1', 'true', or 'yes' to enable automatic credential persistence. When enabled, credentials are saved to .credentials.json (0600 permissions) and auto-loaded on next startup. Default: disabled (in-memory only).\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"sensitive\": false\n    }","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"skill.json","start_line":53,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-08d8588fe0964f2b85b63cfd8feddeee","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.87,"location":{"file":"skill.json","start_line":70,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"The storage model explicitly allows auto-save/auto-load of Bilibili session cookies from .credentials.json when persistence is enabled. Because these cookies can be replayed against official Bilibili endpoints, local disclosure of the file could result in account takeover within the scope of the stored session.","remediation":"Keep the default as in-memory only and remove auto-load persistence unless there is a strong product need. If persistence remains, protect the file with encryption at rest, shorten expiry, require explicit per-session consent, and provide clear warnings plus easy secure deletion.","code_snippet":"      \"revocation\": \"Log out from bilibili.com or clear cookies to invalidate the credential at any time.\"\n    },\n    \"credential_scope_warning\": \"These are Bilibili session cookies. Although the skill only uses them against official Bilibili endpoints listed in `scope_limitations.outbound_destinations`, the cookies themselves — if they leak outside this process — could be replayed against Bilibili by other software. Use a test/throwaway account and rotate cookies after use.\",\n    \"storage\": \"in-memory by default. Set persist=True (or env BILIBILI_PERSIST=1) to auto-save/load credentials from .credentials.json with 0600 permissions. The file is ignored if older than 30 days, refused if the containing directory is group/world-writable, and can be removed via auth.clear_persisted().\",\n    \"security_notes\": [\n      \"Credentials are NOT persisted to disk by default. Persistence must be explicitly opted-in via persist=True or BILIBILI_PERSIST=1.\",\n      \"When persisted, the credential file is written with mode 0600, refused in unsafe directories, and auto-expires after 30 days.\",","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"skill.json","start_line":70,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-261e768f3a37440f9988d8694ef2fdc1","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.86,"location":{"file":"skill.md","start_line":158,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"This documents automatic persistence of full Bilibili session cookies to a predictable local file (`.credentials.json`). Even with `0600` permissions, storing reusable browser session cookies on disk materially increases the attack surface through local compromise, accidental backup/sync leakage, or mishandling in shared/containerized environments.","remediation":"Do not persist full session cookies by default and avoid promoting automatic disk storage for them. Prefer OS keychains/secret managers, require explicit per-user opt-in with strong warnings, store outside the project directory, and implement encryption-at-rest or short-lived token alternatives where possible.","code_snippet":"```\n\nWhen persistence is enabled:\n- Credentials are auto-saved to `.credentials.json` (with `0600` permissions) after initialization\n- On next startup, credentials are auto-loaded from this file\n- You can toggle persistence at runtime: `app.auth.persist = True` / `app.auth.persist = False`\n- To delete the persisted file: `app.auth.clear_persisted()`","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"skill.md","start_line":158,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-503c5fb7693a470a830d319fbc0b355f","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.83,"location":{"file":"skill.md","start_line":177,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"This section explicitly states that credentials can be persisted to `.credentials.json` and manually saved with `auth.save_to_file()`, confirming support for disk storage of full browser session cookies. In the context of a publishing/downloading skill that accepts broad account cookies, persistence makes compromise more damaging because theft of the file can grant account access beyond the intended action.","remediation":"Replace file persistence with secure secret storage mechanisms, or at minimum gate it behind explicit consent, non-default configuration, and prominent risk warnings. Limit scope where possible, avoid saving in working directories, and provide secure deletion/rotation guidance for any stored credentials.","code_snippet":"| **Which features require authentication?** | Publishing (upload/edit/schedule/draft), downloading 1080p+/4K quality videos |\n| **Which features work WITHOUT credentials?** | Hot monitoring, standard-quality downloads, subtitle listing, danmaku fetching, stats viewing |\n| **Where are credentials sent?** | To official Bilibili API endpoints (`api.bilibili.com`, `member.bilibili.com`) over HTTPS only |\n| **Are credentials persisted to disk?** | **NO** by default — credentials stay in memory. Set `persist=True` or `BILIBILI_PERSIST=1` to opt-in to automatic persistence (saved to `.credentials.json` with `0600` permissions). You can also manually call `auth.save_to_file()` |\n| **File permissions for saved credentials** | `0600` (owner read/write only) — restrictive by default |\n\n### Best Practices","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"skill.md","start_line":177,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-f2edb34c365a4d7e8ad81ce81528dadc","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.82,"location":{"file":"src/auth.py","start_line":19,"end_line":null},"finding":"credentials.json","explanation":"The module supports persisting live Bilibili session credentials to a predictable file within the project tree. Even though the code applies permission checks and 0600 file mode, storing reusable session cookies on disk increases the attack surface: local compromise, accidental inclusion in backups, repo packaging, or workspace exposure can lead to account takeover for authenticated actions such as publishing.","remediation":"Prefer in-memory credentials by default and require explicit opt-in for persistence. Store secrets in a dedicated OS keychain/credential vault where possible, place persisted files outside project directories, and ensure the credential path is excluded from version control, archives, and logs.","code_snippet":"# Default path for persisted credentials (relative to project root)\nDEFAULT_CREDENTIAL_FILE = os.path.join(\n    os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))),\n    \".credentials.json\",\n)\n\n# Maximum age (in seconds) of a persisted credential file before it is","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"07a0b37ad28a14919446682ad9b1f38223a950eb02c424e48b49fe75c25268c0","occurrences":[{"file":"src/auth.py","start_line":19,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-30f9fbb254ac47e3ab2d3a4248d4c2ad","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"src/auth.py","start_line":140,"end_line":null},"finding":"chmod 600 %s\",\n                filepath, st.st_mode & 0o777","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"        if hasattr(os, \"geteuid\") and (st.st_mode & (stat.S_IRGRP | stat.S_IROTH)):\n            _logger.warning(\n                \"Ignoring credential file %s: permissions %o allow other users \"\n                \"to read it. Fix with: chmod 600 %s\",\n                filepath, st.st_mode & 0o777, filepath,\n            )\n            return","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"15d8f2e040ed8683cb57c84d811fb96412dab7cfffdacbe696a7f49b28c54943","occurrences":[{"file":"src/auth.py","start_line":140,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-3","finding_id":"finding-a7985ff3729f43479ec6520404a1c961","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":4,"end_line":4},"finding":null,"explanation":"The file presents the skill description entirely in Chinese and labels this README as the Chinese documentation, while the skill is described as a general-purpose Bilibili tool rather than a region-locked compliance tool. Under the policy rule, forcing a specific language without user opt-in or an explicit justified locale constraint is a natural-language policy violation.","remediation":"State clearly that the skill supports multiple languages or provide an explicit user language choice. If the skill is intended only for Chinese-speaking or region-specific use, document that limitation and its justification in the README.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":4,"end_line":4}]},{"id":"LP3","finding_id":"finding-4ecdb0bc0ddd4ab1919bc9a30ab6dde7","category":"MCP Least Privilege","pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"Without declared permissions the skill's intent is opaque and cannot be validated.","remediation":"Declare the skill's tool scope: for Claude Code / Agent Skills SKILL.md, list the tools the skill may invoke in the 'allowed-tools' frontmatter field; for MCP server manifests, add a 'permissions' list naming the required capabilities.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SC4","finding_id":"finding-f43cb19059db4fe19fd4759480c85776","category":"Supply Chain","pattern":"Known Vulnerable Dependency: requests==2.32.3 — 4 advisory(ies): CVE-2024-47081 (Requests vulnerable to .netrc credentials leak via malicious URLs); CVE-2026-25645 (Requests has Insecure Temp File Reuse in its extract_zipped_paths() utility func); CVE-2024-47081 (Requests vulnerable to .netrc credentials leak via malicious URLs) +1 more","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"requirements.txt","start_line":5,"end_line":null},"finding":"requests==2.32.3","explanation":"requests 2.32.3 is reported as affected by advisories including .netrc credential leakage via malicious URLs. This matters more in a skill that makes outbound HTTPS requests and may run in environments with developer or user credentials present, because attacker-influenced URLs or redirects could potentially cause unintended credential disclosure or unsafe file handling in affected utility code.","remediation":"Upgrade requests to a patched version addressing the cited advisories and review any code paths that accept user-controlled URLs or follow redirects. Disable or tightly control .netrc usage where not needed, validate destination hosts, and avoid vulnerable helper utilities for archive extraction.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["Supply Chain"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"083a254d8f9c929b5f3eeec102e186df81a799b86c8422f6a4f7a1ca2f48f05d","occurrences":[{"file":"requirements.txt","start_line":5,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM3","finding_id":"finding-dfde9011c8454795af3bde2c44dfe042","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Unsafe Defaults","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"skill.json","start_line":70,"end_line":null},"finding":"world-writable","explanation":"Tool defaults are unsafe or overly permissive (e.g. disabled TLS verification, no authentication, world-writable permissions). Unsafe defaults widen the attack surface.","remediation":"Override unsafe defaults with secure settings (verify=True, auth required, restrictive permissions). Review and harden all tool configurations.","code_snippet":"    \"\"\"Refuse to write credentials into world/group-accessible locations.\n\n    Guards against \"Memory and Context Poisoning\": if the project directory\n    is shared (e.g. /tmp, a world-writable CI workspace, or a network share),\n    another process could read or replace the saved credentials.\n    \"\"\"\n    directory = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(filepath)) or \".\"","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"f239fe898e7090985596902b5111ef3fb3133f3991fb13b1656c70b3bbdc433f","occurrences":[{"file":"skill.json","start_line":70,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM3","finding_id":"finding-dfde9011c8454795af3bde2c44dfe042","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Unsafe Defaults","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"src/auth.py","start_line":34,"end_line":null},"finding":"world-writable","explanation":"Tool defaults are unsafe or overly permissive (e.g. disabled TLS verification, no authentication, world-writable permissions). Unsafe defaults widen the attack surface.","remediation":"Override unsafe defaults with secure settings (verify=True, auth required, restrictive permissions). Review and harden all tool configurations.","code_snippet":"    \"\"\"Refus